We describe the natural bijection (the tensor–hom adjunction)

$$\Phi:\operatorname{Hom}_{A}\!\left(M\otimes_{A}N,\,P\right)\;\xrightarrow{\;\sim\;}\;\operatorname{Hom}_{A}\!\left(M,\,\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P)\right).$$

Here $A$ is a commutative ring and $M,N,P$ are $A$-modules.

---

**Construction of the map.**

Fix $f\in\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M\otimes_{A}N,\,P)$. We must produce an $A$-linear map $\Phi(f):M\to\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P)$.

For each $m\in M$, define

$$\bigl[\Phi(f)\bigr](m)\;:\;N\longrightarrow P,\qquad n\;\longmapsto\; f(m\otimes n).$$

*Check that $[\Phi(f)](m)$ is $A$-linear in $n$.* For $n,n'\in N$ and $a\in A$,

$$[\Phi(f)](m)(n+n')=f(m\otimes(n+n'))=f(m\otimes n+m\otimes n')=f(m\otimes n)+f(m\otimes n'),$$

$$[\Phi(f)](m)(a\,n)=f(m\otimes a\,n)=f\bigl(a(m\otimes n)\bigr)=a\,f(m\otimes n)=a\,[\Phi(f)](m)(n),$$

where we used bilinearity of $\otimes$ and $A$-linearity of $f$. Hence $[\Phi(f)](m)\in\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P)$.

*Check that $\Phi(f):M\to\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P)$ is $A$-linear in $m$.* For $m,m'\in M$ and $a\in A$,

$$[\Phi(f)](m+m')(n)=f((m+m')\otimes n)=f(m\otimes n)+f(m'\otimes n)=[\Phi(f)](m)(n)+[\Phi(f)](m')(n),$$

$$[\Phi(f)](a\,m)(n)=f(a\,m\otimes n)=f(a(m\otimes n))=a\,f(m\otimes n)=a\,[\Phi(f)](m)(n),$$

where the last step uses that $\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P)$ is an $A$-module with pointwise scalar multiplication. Thus $\Phi(f)\in\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M,\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P))$.

So $\Phi$ is a well-defined map between the two Hom-sets.

---

**The inverse map.**

Given $g\in\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M,\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P))$, define a map

$$\widetilde{g}:M\times N\longrightarrow P,\qquad (m,n)\longmapsto g(m)(n).$$

This is $A$-**bilinear**: $A$-linearity in $n$ follows from $g(m)\in\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P)$; $A$-linearity in $m$ follows from $g$ being $A$-linear and the $A$-module structure on $\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P)$ being pointwise. Explicitly, for $a\in A$,

$$\widetilde{g}(a\,m,n)=g(a\,m)(n)=\bigl(a\,g(m)\bigr)(n)=a\,g(m)(n)=a\,\widetilde{g}(m,n)=\widetilde{g}(m,a\,n),$$

which also shows the balanced condition $\widetilde{g}(a\,m,n)=\widetilde{g}(m,a\,n)$.

By the **universal property of the tensor product**, every $A$-bilinear map $M\times N\to P$ factors uniquely through an $A$-linear map $M\otimes_{A}N\to P$. Hence there is a unique $\Psi(g)\in\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M\otimes_{A}N,\,P)$ satisfying

$$\Psi(g)(m\otimes n)=g(m)(n)\qquad\text{for all }m\in M,\;n\in N.$$

This gives a map $\Psi:\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M,\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P))\to\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M\otimes_{A}N,\,P)$.

---

**The two maps are mutually inverse.**

For $f\in\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M\otimes_{A}N,\,P)$ and every pure tensor $m\otimes n$,

$$\Psi(\Phi(f))(m\otimes n)=[\Phi(f)](m)(n)=f(m\otimes n).$$

Since pure tensors generate $M\otimes_{A}N$, $\Psi(\Phi(f))=f$. Thus $\Psi\circ\Phi=\mathrm{id}$.

Conversely, for $g\in\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M,\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P))$ and every $m\in M,\;n\in N$,

$$[\Phi(\Psi(g))](m)(n)=\Psi(g)(m\otimes n)=g(m)(n).$$

Since the values agree on every $n$, we have $[\Phi(\Psi(g))](m)=g(m)$ for every $m$, hence $\Phi(\Psi(g))=g$. Thus $\Phi\circ\Psi=\mathrm{id}$.

---

**Conclusion.**

The maps

$$\Phi:\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M\otimes_{A}N,\,P)\to\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M,\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P)),\quad f\mapsto\bigl[m\mapsto[n\mapsto f(m\otimes n)]\bigr],$$

$$\Psi:\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M,\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P))\to\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(M\otimes_{A}N,\,P),\quad g\mapsto\bigl[m\otimes n\mapsto g(m)(n)\bigr],$$

are well-defined $A$-linear maps (naturality in all three variables is verified by the same point-evaluation argument) and are mutual inverses. Therefore

$$\operatorname{Hom}_{A}\!\left(M\otimes_{A}N,\,P\right)\;\cong\;\operatorname{Hom}_{A}\!\left(M,\,\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,P)\right),$$

which is the **tensor–hom adjunction** $(-\otimes_{A}N)\dashv\operatorname{Hom}_{A}(N,-)$.

